  "generated_text": "To find the phase shift of the graph of the function \\( y = \\sin (3x - \\pi) \\), we need to analyze the general form of the sine function, which is:\n\n\\[ y = a \\sin(Bx - C) + D \\]\n\nwhere,\n\n- \\( a \\) is the amplitude,\n- \\( B \\) affects the period,\n- \\( C \\) affects the phase shift,\n- \\( D \\) is the vertical shift.\n\nFor the given function \\( y = \\sin (3x - \\pi) \\), we can identify:\n\n- \\( = =  \\)\n\nThe phase shift is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Phase shift} = \\frac{C}{B} \\]\n\n\n, the phase shift is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Phase shift} = \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\]\n\nTherefore, the phase shift of the graph of \\( y = \\sin (3x - \\pi) \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\{\\{\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\}} \\]\n\nThis means the graph is shifted to the right by \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) units.",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[pi/3, '{\\\\{\\\\{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}\\\\}} \\\\]  This means the graph is shifted to the right by \\\\(\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}']",
    "gold_answer": "Since the graph of $y = \\sin (3x - \\pi)$ is the same as the graph of $y = \\sin 3x$ shifted $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ units to the right, the phase shift is $\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}.$\n\n[asy]import TrigMacros;\n\nsize(400);\n\nreal g(real x)\n{\n\treturn sin(3*x - pi);\n}\n\nreal f(real x)\n{\n\treturn sin(3*x);\n}\n\ndraw(graph(g,-2*pi,2*pi,n=700,join=operator ..),red);\ndraw(graph(f,-2*pi,2*pi,n=700,join=operator ..));\ntrig_axes(-2*pi,2*pi,-2,2,pi/2,1);\nlayer();\nrm_trig_labels(-4,4, 2);\n[/asy]\n\nNote that we can also shift the graph of $y = \\sin 3x$ $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ units to the left, so an answer of $\\boxed{-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}$ is also acceptable.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
